ce_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-28T21:43:13.267Z", "request_id": "req_011CgVN4Xrcaw9J3obWY3BY2", "thinking_duration_ms": 27471}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 0}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_start", "index": 1, "content_block": {"type": "text", "text": ""}}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgVN4Y528Yug8jEDG47ST", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I picked **Candidate 7**, but with low confidence. Candidates 0 and 5 are close behind. I worked this in my head from forecast means, without a full day-by-day simulation.\n\n**How the plans differ.** Each order arrives L days later, so I compared each order with the forecast for its arrival day.\n- **Candidate 6** orders about 1.05 × forecast, which is about the average demand (the multiplier averages 1.046).\n- **Scaled versions of the same plan:** Candidate 4 orders about 0.95 × forecast, Candidate 7 about 1.1 ×, Candidate 0 about 1.15 ×, Candidate 1 about 1.2 × and Candidate 3 about 1.3 ×.\n- **Candidate 2** is Candidate 6 with much larger orders on days 0–1 and smaller orders at the end.\n- **Candidate 5** orders every other day, buying two days' worth at once at about 1.1 ×.\n\n**Running out costs more than overstocking.** A missed sale costs 14 per unit for product 0 (9 margin + 5 penalty) and 13 for product 1 (6 + 7). A spare unit costs only holding, and it keeps selling for up to 7 days. Daily demand varies by about 30 %, so a plan that orders exactly the average (Candidate 6) runs short often. That points to a buffer of roughly 10–15 % (Candidates 7 and 0). Going higher (Candidates 1 and 3) builds up stock that eventually spoils at a